"""Allowed 32,768 generated tokens in place of 16,384, one Think-SFT + cue sample gains 6.7 points on the 328 harder problems
(210.8 -> 232.8, mean of 4 samples), but the vote over 4 samples does not gain separably (238 -> 244, interval -0.9 to 4.9).

The page of one finding, served at /finding/cut-off-is-unfinished (view.py serves every tools/finding_<slug>.py) and
reached from its tile on /findings, which ICON draws. The picture is one lit plate. On it, each of the 328 answers of one
run is one thin horizontal bar whose length is the number of tokens it generated, sorted by length (shortest at the top),
on an axis of generated tokens from 0 to 32,768. A bar is blue when the answer ended within the allowance and is right,
grey when it ended and is wrong, and red up to the allowance when it was cut off there; the rest of a cut-off answer is a
faint line to its true end. The counts sit in the empty space above the long bars: right answers of 328 (the large
number), answers cut off, and generated tokens per problem. The one control is a slider, the tokens allowed per answer,
continuous from 16,384 to 32,768: the same stored answers are scored at any allowance (an answer counts as right at
allowance L when it ended within L tokens, was not cut off at 32,768, and grader v3 scored it right). As the allowance
moves right, the cut-off bars reach their end one by one and take the colour of their outcome. A quiet three-way
switch shows Think-SFT + cue, RL or Base + cue; the bars travel to the chosen run's order and lengths. For Think-SFT + cue
the bars draw sample 1 of its 4 samples and the counts are the mean of the 4 (FOUR); a second quiet switch, One sample /
Vote over 4 samples, turns the bars into one per problem, as long as its longest sample and coloured by the vote, which is
scored at 16,384 and 32,768 only (board item result-dd26fe). Beside the picture,
three small bars compare the runs: of each run's answers past 16,384 tokens, how many are right, wrong and still cut off
at the chosen allowance (at 32,768: 23 of 93, 2 of 20, 4 of 64 right). A click on a bar shows that problem, its token
count, its outcome at 16,384, at the chosen allowance and at 32,768, and the stored end of the answer, for all three runs.
Address: /finding/cut-off-is-unfinished#[t<tokens>/][rl/, base/ or vote/][<panel or p<problem>>]; the default, Think-SFT + cue at
32,768, is left out. An empty address opens at 16,384 and travels to 32,768. The rules are in .claude/skills/finding-page/SKILL.md;
the looks are those of tools/architecture.py (.claude/skills/site-ui/SKILL.md). The finding is board item result-c8b994
(work-ceccc0); the Base + cue cell is board item result-0c1eef.

The artifacts are on main: the readout studies/rlneurons/results/cap32/readout.json and its run folders (commit c3481f1;
the base_cue cell commit dcedebb), the per-problem rows results/cap32/rows-01/ (commit 10fc0f5, written by
analysis/cap32_rows.py from the jobs' raw answers, which are not in git) and results/cap32/rows-02/ (Base + cue, commit
8ac77d7, written by the one-off command in its run.json from the 4 shards joined), and the section 'Token cap 32,768 on the 328' of studies/rlneurons/README.md. PROBLEMS
below is built from the rows and the problem texts of the 328; every count on the page is computed from PROBLEMS. The
deployed site ships only tools/*.py, so the data is a literal; running this file compares it with the artifacts.

    PYTHONPATH=. uv run --no-project --with numpy python tools/finding_cut_off_is_unfinished.py
        checks DATA, LINE and PROBLEMS against the readout, the rows, the run folders and RL's earlier samples, and
        recomputes the paired bootstrap intervals
    ... python tools/finding_cut_off_is_unfinished.py --write    rewrites PROBLEMS from the rows
"""
